o two halves, tests each half. If a half fails, continue with that half; else test complement. Continue until cannot reduce further.\n\nWe need to count the number of tests performed.\n\nThe output minimal_failure is the final minimal set (list of items). Outcome is the result of test on that minimal set (should be \"FAIL\"). Tests is the number of test invocations.\n\nBut we also need to output minimal_failure as a list of strings. The order? Probably the same order as in items? Or alphabetical? The fixtures don't specify order. We can return the minimal set in the order they appear in items (or sorted? but no requirement). We'll keep the order as found.\n\nGiven the fixtures, we can test our implementation.\n\nLet's simulate for case 0:\nitems = [d0,d1,d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7]\nrequired = [d2,d3]\nunresolved = []\nFull set: FAIL. Test 1.\nDelta debugging algorithm: usually split into two subsets: e.g., first half [d0,d1,d2,d3], second half [d4,d5,d6,d7]. Test first half: contains d2,d3 => FAIL. Test 2. So we continue with first half. Split first half: [d0,d1] and [d2,d3]. Test [d0,d1]: does not contain both required -> PASS. Test 3. Test [d2,d3]: contains both -> FAIL. Test 4. Continue with [d2,d3]. Split [d2,d3] into [d2] and [d3]. Test [d2]: not both -> PASS. Test 5. Test [d3]: not both -> PASS. Test 6. No further reduction, minimal is [d2,d3]. Tests = 6. minimal_failure = [\"d2\",\"d3\"] in order? That matches expected? The fixture doesn't give expected output, but that seems plausible.\n\nCase 1: required=[d3,d4], unresolved=[d0]. Full set: FAIL. Test 1. Split: first half [d0,d1,d2,d3] (contains d3 but not d4 -> PASS? Actually required are d3 and d4, so first half does not have d4 => PASS). Second half [d4,d5,d6,d7] (contains d4 but not d3 => PASS). But both halves PASS? Then algorithm tests complement? Actually delta debugging: if neither half fails, test the complement of each half? Standard algorithm: after splitting, if a subset fails, continue with it. If none fails, combine subsets? Actually the classic delta debugging for failing test case: start with full set C. Let n=2. Split C into n subsets. Test each subset. If any subset fails, set C to that subset, n=2. If no subset fails, test the complements (i.e., combinations of subsets). If any complement fails, set C to that complement, n=2. Else, increase granularity: n=2n, and repeat. Continue until no reduction possible.\n\nIn this case, subsets of size 4: S1=[d0,d1,d2,d3] (missing d4) => PASS. S2=[d4,d5,d6,d7] (missing d3) => PASS. So no subset fails. Then test complements: complement of S1 is S2? Actually complement of S1 is S2? Wait, complement of S1 (relative to full set) is S2. So we already tested S2. The algorithm tests complements of each subset, which are unions of all other subsets. Since there are only two subsets, complement of S1 is S2, already tested. So no complement fails. So n=4, split into 4 subsets of size 2: [d0,d1], [d2,d3], [d4,d5], [d6,d7]. Test each. Test [d0,d1]: PASS (no required). Test [d2,d3]: contains d3 only -> PASS. Test [d4,d5]: contains d4 only -> PASS. Test [d6,d7]: PASS. No subset fails. Then test complements (i.e., unions of 3 subsets). Complement of [d0,d1] is rest: [d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7] – contains both d3 and d4? Yes, d3 and d4 are present => FAIL. Test that complement: FAIL. So set C = complement = [d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7]. n=2 again. Now split C into 2 subsets: S1=[d2,d3,d4] (contains both required? d3,d4 present => FAIL). So we take S1. Then split S1: [d2] and [d3,d4]. Test [d2]: PASS. Test [d3,d4]: contains both => FAIL. So C=[d3,d4]. Split: [d3] and [d4]. Test [d3]: PASS. Test [d4]: PASS. No reduction. Minimal set = [d3,d4]. But note unresolved_without includes d0. In our test, we never tested a set without d0? In the subset [d3,d4], d0 is missing, but unresolved_without says if d0 is missing, test is unresolved. So we need to incorporate that. So when testing [d3,d4], since d0 is missing, test is unresolved. That means it cannot be considered a valid FAIL. So the algorithm must only consider sets that include all unresolved_without. Therefore, during minimization, we must ensure we never remove unresolved_without items. So we should start from full set, but only consider subsets that still contain all unresolved_without. So we must ensure that d0 is always present. So the minimal set must include d0. So the minimal failing set will be {d0, d3, d4}? Because required are d3,d4, and unresolved_without d0 must be present. But does {d0,d3,d4} fail? Yes, it contains required and has d0. So the minimal set should be {d0,d3,d4}. But is it possible to reduce further? Can we remove d0? No because unresolved. Can we remove d3? Then fails. So minimal is {d0,d3,d4}. That fits the pattern: the minimal_failure should include all unresolved_without items plus the required_failure_items, but maybe some of required could be removed? No, both required are necessary.\n\nSo our algorithm must treat unresolved_without as items that must be present in any valid test set. That means we cannot test any set that lacks those items, and we must treat such tests as invalid (maybe treat as PASS for algorithmic purposes? Or we could skip them). In delta debugging, we always start from full set which includes all, then when we split, we should only consider subsets that still contain all unresolved_without. That means we cannot split arbitrarily. For example, in case 1, split into two halves: first half [d0,d1,d2,d3] contains d0, so valid; second half [d4,d5,d6,d7] does not contain d0, so invalid. So we cannot test the second half because set must include d0. So the algorithm must adjust: we only split into subsets that include all unresolved_without. One way is to treat unresolved_without as fixed part of the set, and we only attempt to reduce the rest. So the set to minimize is the items excluding unresolved_without? Actually the minimal failing set must include unresolved_without, so we can consider removing only other items. So we can fix the unresolved_without items as part of the base set, and then try to remove some of the remaining items to see if the set still fails. The delta debugging can be applied to the set of items that are not in unresolved_without, but the test always includes the unresolved_without items. So the algorithm: initial set C = items (including all). Then we fix the unresolved_without items as \"required\" for valid test, so we treat them as always present. So we can define a helper function test(S_subset) that tests the set S = S_subset ∪ unresolved_without. And we want to find minimal subset of (items - unresolved_without) such that test FAILs (i.e., the set contains all required_failure_items). The minimal_failure output should be that minimal S (including unresolved_without). That matches case 1: unresolved = {d0}, remaining items = {d1,d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7}. Required = {d3,d4}. The minimal failing subset of remaining items is {d3,d4} because removing d3 or d4 makes it fail? Actually we need both. So minimal overall set = {d0,d3,d4}. That seems plausible.\n\nCase 2: required=[d4,d7], unresolved=[d0]. Then fix d0. Minimal failing set should be {d0,d4,d7}. Good.\n\nCase 0: unresolved=[], so no fixed items. Minimal set = required. That matches.\n\nThus the algorithm: Let base = set(unresolved_without). Let variable_items = [item for item in items if item not in base]. We need to find the smallest subset of variable_items (in terms of inclusion minimal) such that when combined with base, the set contains all required_failure_items. This is essentially finding the minimal hitting set? Actually required_failure_items are items that must be present. So the condition is: base ∪ subset must contain all required_failure_items. So we need to find a minimal set of variable_items that, together with base, covers required_failure_items. That is, we need to pick items from variable_items to cover the required items that are not already in base. Since base may also contain some required items, we only need to add missing required items. So the minimal set is simply the set of required_failure_items that are not in base. Because any additional variable items are unnecessary. So minimal_failure = base ∪ (required_failure_items - base). That is exactly what we hypothesized: base + required missing from base. That seems too trivial. But why would we need delta debugging? The output includes tests count, which suggests we need to perform a search. However, maybe the test function is more complex: it might be that the test result depends on interactions, not just set containment. The required_failure_items might be items that individually cause failure? Or maybe the test function is unknown and we must discover it via delta debugging on the given case? But the case provides the test definition via the payload fields. The payload provides items, required_failure_items, unresolved_without. That seems to define the test behavior exactly. So why would we need to simulate delta debugging? Because the output requires tests (number of test invocations). That implies we must actually perform the search algorithm and count tests. The minimal_failure can be computed directly, but the algorithm will perform tests to find it. So we need to simulate the delta debugging process, counting test calls, and then output the minimal set found. That minimal set should be the same as the direct computation (base ∪ required missing from base). But the algorithm might find that even with a different number of tests.\n\nThus, our solve function should implement the delta debugging algorithm as described, with the test function that checks if the set (including base) contains all required_failure_items. But we must also account for unresolved_without: any set that does not include all unresolved_without is considered invalid/unresolved. In the algorithm, we should only test sets that include all unresolved_without. So we can treat the test as: given a candidate set S (list of items), if all unresolved_without are in S, then return \"FAIL\" if all required_failure_items are in S, else \"PASS\". If any unresolved_without is missing, return \"UNRESOLVED\". But for algorithm, we treat unresolved sets as not failing (i.e., cannot reduce to them). So we will only consider subsets that contain all unresolved_without.\n\nIn delta debugging, we start with full set = items (which includes base). Then we try to minimize by splitting only in ways that keep base. One way: treat base as fixed, and apply delta debugging to the variable_items. So the set to minimize is variable list. The test function for a subset X of variable_items is: test_set = base ∪ X. Then return \"FAIL\" if base ∪ X contains all required_failure_items; else \"PASS\". This is a deterministic test. Then we apply standard delta debugging (minimization algorithm) on the list variable_items, using this test function, to find a minimal subset X that still fails (i.e., the test_set fails). The minimal_failure will be base + X (as list). The outcome is \"FAIL\". The tests count is the number of times test function is called.\n\nWe need to implement the delta debugging algorithm. The classic algorithm by Zeller for minimizing a failing input is:\n\ndef ddmin(case):\n    def test(ss):\n        # return 'FAIL' if the set ss causes failure else 'PASS'\n    n = 2\n    c = case  # list of items\n    while True:\n        # split c into n subsets\n        subsets = [c[i*len(c)//n:(i+1)*len(c)//n] for i in range(n)]\n        # test each subset\n        any_fail = False\n        for s in subsets:\n            if test(s) == 'FAIL':\n                c = s\n                n = 2\n                any_fail = True\n                break\n        if any_fail:\n            continue\n        # test complements\n        for s in subsets:\n            complement = [x for x in c if x not in s]\n            if complement and test(complement) == 'FAIL':\n                c = complement\n                n = max(n-1, 2)  # actually reduce n to 2? Typically set n = n-1? Standard algorithm sets n = n-1? I recall: if a complement fails, set c = complement and n = n-1 (or 2). Better to follow standard.\n                any_fail = True\n                break\n        if any_fail:\n            continue\n        if n == len(c):\n            break\n        n = min(2*n, len(c))\n    return c\n\nBut we need to track number of tests. Also we need to ensure we don't test empty set or set missing base. Since we are only dealing with variable_items, we can pass variable_items list to ddmin, and the test function will add base.\n\nFor the test function, we need to count calls.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Extract items, required_failure_items, unresolved_without from case.\n- Convert to sets for efficient checks.\n- base = set(unresolved_without)\n- variable = [item for item in items if item not in base]\n- required_set = set(required_failure_items)\n- test_count = 0\n- define test(subset_variable):\n    test_count += 1\n    test_set = base.union(subset_variable)\n    if required_set.issubset(test_set):\n        return 'FAIL'\n    else:\n        return 'PASS'\n- Run ddmin on variable list. But note: variable list may contain items that are in required_set as well. The test function will check.\n- After ddmin, minimal_variable = the result list c (subset of variable).\n- minimal_failure = list(base) + minimal_variable (order? We need to return list. We can return base in original order? The original order of items is given. We can keep order: iterate over items, and include if in base or minimal_variable set. That preserves order.\n- outcome = 'FAIL' (since the final test fails; we could re-test to confirm, but we already know).\n- tests = test_count\n\nBut we should also handle edge cases: If test on full variable set (i.e., all variable) fails? It should because full set includes all items, so includes required. But if base already contains all required, then any subset will also fail? Actually if required is subset of base, then test on empty variable fails. Then minimal_variable should be empty. So ddmin must handle that. The initial c = variable list. If test(empty) fails? But we start with full list. In ddmin, we test subsets; if test(empty) is never called because empty subset may occur? It could if we split and one subset is empty? We should avoid testing empty set because it might be invalid? But test(empty) would test base only, which fails if required subset of base. That's valid. So we should allow empty set. In splitting, if len(c) == 0, we stop. Also if n > len(c), we set n=len(c). So we need to handle that.\n\nWe'll implement standard ddmin with modifications to avoid infinite loops. We'll follow the algorithm from the original paper or common implementations.\n\nLet’s write code:\n\ndef ddmin(a_list):\n    n = 2\n    c = a_list[:]\n    while True:\n        len_c = len(c)\n        if len_c == 0:\n            break\n        # split into n subsets\n        subsets = [c[i*len_c//n:(i+1)*len_c//n] for i in range(n)]\n        # test each subset\n        failed = False\n        for s in subsets:\n            if test(s) == 'FAIL':\n                c = s\n                n = 2\n                failed = True\n                break\n        if failed:\n            continue\n        # test complements\n        for s in subsets:\n            comp = [x for x in c if x not in s]\n            if len(comp) == 0:\n                continue\n            if test(comp) == 'FAIL':\n                c = comp\n                n = max(n-1, 2)  # sometimes set n = n-1; but to be safe, n = 2?\n                failed = True\n                break\n        if failed:\n            continue\n        if n == len_c:\n            break\n        n = min(n*2, len_c)\n    return c\n\nBut note: the standard algorithm often sets n = n-1 when complement fails. However, for simplicity and correctness, setting n=2 is fine because we are starting over. But it might increase test count. We need to match expected results. Since we don't have expected test counts, we can choose a standard version. I'll use the version where after complement success, n = n-1 (but at least 2). Or n=2? Let's see the algorithm from the book: \n1. Start with full set C, n=2.\n2. Partition C into n subsets.\n3. If any subset fails, set C to that subset, n=2, go to step2.\n4. Else, test complements (each subset's complement). If any complement fails, set C to that complement, set n = n-1 (but not less than 2), go to step2.\n5. Else, if n == |C|, stop.\n   Else, double n, go to step2.\n\nThat's the typical algorithm. I'll implement that.\n\nAlso, we need to ensure we don't infinite loop if n doubles beyond len(C). We limit n to len(C).\n\nNow, test function will use global test_count variable. We'll define solve(case) with locals.\n\nOne more nuance: The variable list may contain duplicates? Items list is unique? Probably yes. We'll treat as list.\n\nNow, after ddmin, we have minimal_variable list. Then minimal_failure = sorted order? The order in which they appear in original items list. We'll construct:\nminimal_failure = [item for item in items if item in base or item in minimal_variable_set]\nThis preserves original order.\n\noutcome = \"FAIL\" (always, because we only reduce if test fails; if base alone already fails, then minimal_variable empty and test(empty) fails, so outcome is FAIL).\ntests = test_count\n\nReturn dict.\n\nEdge case: If test on full set fails? It should. If not, maybe the case is invalid? But fixtures assume it fails.\n\nNow, let's simulate case 0 with this algorithm to see test count.\n\nCase 0: base = set(), variable = d0-d7. required = {d2,d3}.\nFull set test(0..7) -> FAIL (count=1)\nn=2, split: [d0,d1,d2,d3] test -> FAIL (count=2) -> c=[d0,d1,d2,d3], n=2.\nsplit: [d0,d1] test -> PASS (count=3), [d2,d3] test -> FAIL (count=4) -> c=[d2,d3], n=2.\nsplit: [d2] test -> PASS (count=5), [d3] test -> PASS (count=6) -> both PASS. Then complements: complement of [d2] is [d3] test -> PASS (count=7), complement of [d3] is [d2] test -> PASS (count=8). No complement fails, n == len(c) (2==2) -> stop. So c=[d2,d3]. Tests = 8? The earlier manual count was 6. I miscounted. Actually in my earlier manual, I didn't complement tests after both subsets PASS. The algorithm requires testing complements. So total tests: initial 1, then subsets 2, then subsets 2 more (total 5? Let's recount):\n1: test(full) = FAIL (1)\n2: test([d0,d1,d2,d3]) = FAIL (2)\n3: test([d0,d1]) = PASS (3)\n4: test([d2,d3]) = FAIL (4) -> set c=[d2,d3], n=2\n5: test([d2]) = PASS (5)\n6: test([d3]) = PASS (6)\nNow both subsets PASS, so test complements:\n7: test(complement of [d2] = [d3]) = PASS (7)\n8: test(complement of [d3] = [d2]) = PASS (8)\nn==len(c) -> stop. So 8 tests. That seems plausible.\n\nCase 1: base=[d0], variable = list of d1..d7 (7 items). required = {d3,d4}. Let's simulate quickly:\n- test(full variable set = d1..d7) -> with base, set contains d3,d4? Yes, FAIL (count=1)\nn=2, split variable into 3/4? len(variable)=7, n=2, subsets: sizes 3 and 4. First subset [d1,d2,d3] test: base+[d1,d2,d3] contains d3 but not d4 => PASS (count=2). Second subset [d4,d5,d6,d7] test: base+[d4,d5,d6,d7] contains d4 but not d3 => PASS (count=3). Both PASS, so test complements:\ncomplement of first subset = [d4,d5,d6,d7] (already tested? But we test complement directly: test([d4,d5,d6,d7]) = PASS (count=4) (same as before). Complement of second = [d1,d2,d3] test = PASS (count=5). No complement fails. Now n=2*2=4? Since n < len(c), double n=4. Split variable into 4 subsets: sizes: 2,2,2,1. Subsets: [d1,d2] test: base+[d1,d2] -> missing both => PASS (6); [d3,d4] test: base+[d3,d4] -> contains both => FAIL (7) -> set c=[d3,d4] (variable subset), n=2.\nNow c=[d3,d4] (variable subset). Test subsets: [d3] test: base+[d3] -> contains d3 but not d4 => PASS (8); [d4] test: base+[d4] -> PASS (9). Complements: complement of [d3] = [d4] test -> PASS (10); complement of [d4] = [d3] test -> PASS (11). n==len(c)=2 -> stop. So minimal_variable = [d3,d4] (order as in variable? likely [d3,d4] from split). minimal_failure = [d0] + [d3,d4] but order in original items: d0,d1,d2,d3,d4,... so [d0,d3,d4] but note d3 before d4? In original list d3 before d4. So output should maintain that order: [d0,d3,d4]. Tests = 11.\n\nCase 2 similar. So this seems plausible.\n\nTherefore we implement the algorithm as described.\n\nOne more thing: The output requirements: minimal_failure array of strings, outcome string \"FAIL\", tests integer.\n\nWe need to ensure that we count tests accurately. We'll increment count on every call to test.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use list for variable_items.\n- Use set for base and required.\n- Define test as inner function that modifies nonlocal count.\n- Implement ddmin as inner function that calls test.\n\nPotential issues: If variable list is empty, the algorithm should still work. If base alone fails (required subset of base), then test(empty_variable) should be called at the start? But we start with full variable set (empty), so c = [] . Then len(c)=0, algorithm would break immediately? We need to handle: if variable is empty, then the minimal set is base only. The test on empty variable should be performed to confirm failure? But we could just return base and outcome \"FAIL\" with tests = 1 (the initial test on full set which is base). So we need to handle that.\n\nOur ddmin expects a list; if empty, the while True: len_c = len(c) = 0, then break, returning [] . So we need to have test_count start at 1 by calling test on full variable before ddmin? Actually we should test the full set first to ensure it fails. In algorithm, we assume the full set fails. But we should still count that test. So we can call test(variable) initially, and if it does not fail, maybe the outcome is PASS? But given the problem, it should always fail. We'll still count that initial test. So we set c = variable, and call te